PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.2/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: Solid, well-labelled set. Intents match the boards, the mechanic ladder is clean (fill, overlap, reach, colour, void, stamp, colour-cross, smudged, cracked, chain, combos) and trap rate is 0 everywhere. Weaknesses: some mid-size pictures are hard to read or carry stray cells (Whale, Turtle, Horse, Pond, Laurel, Compass); the cracked, chain and smudged teach levels already sit at 55-65% casual wins; chapters 1-2 lean on exact-fit/overlap openings so many levels feel alike; and after L81 there are long runs at 55-61% with only a few breathers. Difficulty targets are met overall (L1-3 at 100/100/93%, no more than two easy levels in a row, last-third casual average about 63%). Curve: Teach, practise, twist, boss and finale repeat consistently, and the breathers (L7, L19, L31, L43, L55, L67, L79, L91, L103, L115) are easier than their neighbours. Mechanic order is right: fill/overlap/exact-fit, reach, colour, void, stamp, colour-cross, smudged, cracked, chain, then combos. Problems: (1) Chapters 1-2 run 24 levels on only 3-4 ideas, so the openings repeat. (2) Colour (L25) gets one line of teaching on touching colours. (3) From L85, early practise levels drop to 57-66% (L87-L90, L99-L101), steeper than their labels imply. Soften them to about 70% or put the easiest cracked and chain boards first. (4) L48 (67%) and L60 (69%) are the only mid-game spikes. (5) L81-L84 and L99-L101 give runs of four or more levels at 55-61% with no relief. Add an easy 80%+ board inside each run.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L12 (6/10): Whale picture is unclear: three isolated spout cells and ragged tail columns. It is only 82% casual, which is soft for a finale. The aha repeats earlier exact-fit plus overlap. → Redraw the whale with a clear body curve and a proper spout. Make the finale chain two deductions: X's from the exact row, then column 1's reach, so it feels like a capstone.
L41 (6/10): Laurel's centre void is a big empty block (about 25 cells) and most rows are tiny edge runs, so the board wastes space and plays as a thin chain. 77% casual is hard for a twist. → Shrink the central void to 3x3 and fill the wreath with leaf pairs so more lines overlap.
L42 (6/10): Horse is unreadable at 13x12, with void scraps all over and 73% casual for a mid-game boss. → Redraw as a clearer profile, make the void a clean diagonal, and aim for about 78%.
L81 (6/10): Firefly jar: 61% casual and a smudge with nothing given makes rows noisy and almost guess-prone; the hidden column's colours are scattered. → Give the jar's sides a regular pattern and reduce scattered fireflies.
L82 (6/10): Lantern boat is 59% casual and the edge decision comes from column 1 and 13 clues; unclear for casual players. → Add a hint line or move the edge decision to a cleaner column.
L83 (6/10): Pinwheel is asymmetric and noisy, and 61% for a combine early in the late chapter. → Reduce vane length differences.
L87 (6/10): The vase is 66% casual, a steep drop from the teach level 75%. → Add a second line that bounds the crack.
L88 (6/10): The willow plate is 61% and 87% skilled; steep for a practise level. → Simplify the willow detail.
L89 (6/10): The twist is shown, but 57% for an early cracked twist is harsh. → Put the ? under a fully determined set of columns so deductions are visible.
L90 (6/10): Two cracks, with 55% casual; harder than the L93 combine that follows. → Drop to one crack.
L99 (6/10): 59% casual at the third chain level; too steep. → Add a free row.
L100 (6/10): 57% casual; the moon's right half is a diagonal flood of cells. → Add a second foothold.
L101 (6/10): The lander is 56%, with an empty hollow that wastes space. → Fill the hollow with a window frame.
L3 (7/10): Jellyfish is tiny and the rows 4-6 are sparse scraps, so the overlap lesson is thin and the picture is weak. → Grow to 6x7 with a fuller bell and two tentacles so rows 1-3 give stronger overlap and the lower rows chain from column 3.
L10 (7/10): Row 8 of the turtle is two stray feet and the shell reads as a blob. Exact fit and overlap are stated but the aha is mechanical. → Add a head and tail to row 8 so it forms a clear flipper line, and break the shell symmetry so one column needs an X before it resolves.
L15 (7/10): The owl is weak; rows 8-10 are scraps and the twig in row 6 is easy to miss. → Give the owl legs and a clear branch, and trim the empty lower-left.
L17 (7/10): Tufts and ear detail add clutter; the 'twist' is just a second anchor. → Remove the lower-left tufts and make the short ear the single twist.
L22 (7/10): Squirrel is very irregular and 34 looks makes it a long filler. Not a real twist. → Simplify the tail curl and shorten the feet runs so the twist is one clean idea.
L24 (7/10): Heron plus pond is cluttered, and the left side (rows 5-9) has empty columns. Its reach hook echoes L13. → Trim the empty columns 4-5 or place a reflection there, and make the heron's beak the unique reach.
L27 (7/10): Carrot is a soft repeat of the L25/L26 touching-colour trick with little new. → Add a case where colour order alone decides a row's gap, such as a row with orange, green, orange, and drop the exact-fit row 2.
L28 (7/10): Watermelon is a good shape but teal rind cells are scattered and bottom-heavy. → Fill the empty top-right corner with a seed or the second slice.
L29 (7/10): Pear stem and leaf at the top are scraps. The twist is mostly one exact row. → Make the leaf a clear brown mass and add a second twist row.
L32 (7/10): Flowerpot is sparse in the middle (rows 7 and 8) and the stem is one column. Two rows hold nearly the whole logic. → Add a leaf cell on the stem so one column is not decided alone.
L35 (7/10): Apple basket has a trivially full row 6 and many repeated weave rows, so it plays as busywork. → Vary the weave and put one apple off-centre so colour cross-checks matter.
L38 (7/10): The 'aha' about the void cutting a clue is real, but the bell reads weak and the top row is a flat bar. → Give the bell a rounded crown and handle.

